course: dictionary
lesson: 2204
title: A face, a mood, and keeping a straight face
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Countenance is an old, formal word for a face, or the look on it. Older books use it often, so you will meet it more in old stories than in everyday talk. «The Badger looked at him very suspiciously, but Toad's frank, open countenance made it difficult to suggest any unworthy motive.» Toad's countenance is his face, and the words frank and open tell you it showed honest feelings.
explain: Use the clues around the word. The Badger was suspicious, yet Toad's face was open and honest, so the Badger could not think of a bad reason for him. Ask: what word would fit here? Try "face" in the sentence instead. It still makes sense, so countenance means face.
explain: Countenance can also be a verb, meaning to allow or approve of something. It is usually used with not: "The teacher would not countenance cheating" means she would never allow cheating. To keep your countenance means to keep a calm, straight face and not laugh, which is what Alice often finds hard when the creatures say silly things.
fact: Countenance (noun) means a person's face, or the look on it.
fact: A cheerful countenance is a happy-looking face, and a grim countenance is a stern, unhappy one.
fact: As a verb, countenance means to allow or approve of something, and it is usually used as "not countenance".
fact: To "keep your countenance" means to keep a calm, straight face and not laugh.
fact: Countenance is formal and old-fashioned, so you will see it most in older books and the Bible.
exercise: «Toad's frank, open countenance made it difficult to suggest any unworthy motive.» What does countenance mean here? | A loud noise | A small, dark room | A reason for doing something | *A face, or the look on it
exercise: A teacher says, «I would not countenance cheating.» What does would not countenance suggest? | She would happily join in | *She would never allow it | She would be surprised by it | She would laugh at it
exercise: Ella says countenance is mid, so just say face. Why might an author choose countenance? | It means happy | *It is a formal, old-fashioned word that gives a serious or old-fashioned feel | It means the same as cheating | It is shorter than face
test: Her «cheerful countenance» showed she was pleased with the news. What is a cheerful countenance? | A loud crowd | A grey, wet sky | *A happy-looking face | A sad song
test: When someone says something silly, how do you keep your countenance? | Cover your whole face with your hands | Turn and run away | Shout loudly at them | *Keep a calm, straight face and do not laugh
exam: Which sentence uses countenance as a verb? | A stern countenance stared back at him | *The head would not countenance any bullying | Her countenance was pale and tired
